      "writtenOn": "2022-03-17T12:53:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I wonder what are the implications of removing the listener on this handler thread but actually running the user callback on another thread? I suppose it means that another callback can be run or scheduled before the user callback completes. Perhaps that\u0027s fine though? It doesn\u0027t seem any different from the case in which the user runs the callback on this handler thread but actually posts a lambda to another thread.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 686,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 688,
        "endChar": 70
      },
      "revId": "83b637f1d45794b53b69a72eaecf461fcd2d3e61",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4d8dd003_a708bf80",
        "filename": "packages/ConnectivityT/framework-t/src/android/net/nsd/NsdManager.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 688,
      "author": {
        "id": 1288034
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-18T10:38:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think it\u0027s possible for another callback to be scheduled after this point, since it would be scheduled from the handler thread, and the listener was removed already from the same handler.\n\nDepending on the Executor used I guess it\u0027s possible for a previous callback to be run at the same time or after onStopDiscoveryFailed (if the Executor doesn\u0027t have any particular order for example) ? But just as callbacks were executed in order before, they are posted in order to the provided Executor now, which I think is the only guarantee that NsdManager can/should give.",
      "parentUuid": "fc0d5a51_64a38e6b",
